<article class="legal-body">
<section id="who" aria-labelledby="who-title">
<h2 id="who-title">Who we are</h2>
<p>YAP Arena (yaparena.com) is operated by <mark class="legal-todo">[Legal name of the business]</mark>, <mark class="legal-todo">[Business address in Québec]</mark> (“YAP Arena”, “we”, “us”). This policy covers yaparena.com and the sign-in, live video and replay services that are part of it.</p>
<p>The person in charge of the protection of personal information at YAP Arena is <mark class="legal-todo">[Name and title of the person in charge]</mark>. You can reach them at <mark class="legal-todo">[Privacy email]</mark>. Under Québec law, this is the person with the highest authority in the business unless that role is delegated in writing.</p>
</section>
<section id="collect" aria-labelledby="collect-title">
<h2 id="collect-title">Information we collect</h2>
<h3>Information you give us</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Account.</strong> Your email address, confirmed by a one-time code or by Google. From Google we take only your email address. We never ask for or store a password.</li>
<li>
<strong>Profile.</strong> Your username, your permanent handle, the avatar you choose and an optional bio, and your confirmation that you are 18 or older and accept the Terms of Use, with its version and time.</li>
<li>
<strong>Debates.</strong> The video and audio of your debates; the transcript of your turns with the time of each word; the AI outline of your arguments and any flags you add to it; the language you debate in and your consent to recording and transcription.</li>
<li>
<strong>Taking part.</strong> Chat messages, likes, reports and appeals, the topics and people you follow, and the challenges, queue entries and times you propose.</li>
<li>
<strong>Yap Coins.</strong> The positions you take in each turn of a debate and their results.</li>
<li>
<strong>Messages to us.</strong> What you write when you contact us.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Information we collect automatically</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Sign-in and security.</strong> A random session identifier in a cookie, and the sign-in methods and activity on your account (shown in your account settings).</li>
<li>
<strong>Device and connection.</strong> Your IP address and browser details reach our hosting and video providers with each request, and they keep them in their logs for security and troubleshooting. We don’t store your IP address in our database.</li>
<li>
<strong>Debating readiness.</strong> Whether your camera and microphone check passed, and whether you are on the site to receive a challenge.</li>
<li>
<strong>Analytics, only if you accept analytics cookies.</strong> Which public pages you open, live and replay watch time, replay starts, matches, follows, reports and return visits. We don’t record your email, handle, IP address, search text, chat or report text in analytics.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Information from others</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Sign-in providers.</strong> Auth0, and Google if you use it, confirm your email address.</li>
<li>
<strong>Other people on YAP Arena.</strong> Reports that name your messages or debates, and moderators’ decisions about them.</li>
<li>
<strong>Our AI providers.</strong> The captions and AI outline made from what you say in a debate.</li>
</ul>
<p>We don’t ask for your date of birth, a payment card, government ID, your contacts or your precise location.</p>
</section>
<section id="use" aria-labelledby="use-title">
<h2 id="use-title">How we use it</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>To run your account</strong>: sign-in, your profile, follows and notifications.</li>
<li>
<strong>To run debates</strong>: matching debaters, carrying live video, recording it and publishing the replay.</li>
<li>
<strong>To caption and outline debates</strong>: speech-to-text for captions, and an AI outline that summarizes each side’s arguments.</li>
<li>
<strong>To run Yap Coins</strong>, the play-money game on each debate.</li>
<li>
<strong>To keep the community safe</strong>: chat, reports, moderation, appeals, rate limits and abuse prevention.</li>
<li>
<strong>To improve YAP Arena</strong>, with analytics cookies only if you accept them.</li>
<li>
<strong>To meet legal obligations</strong> and answer lawful requests.</li>
</ul>
<p>We use your information only for these purposes, or for another purpose you consent to or the law allows.</p>
</section>
<section id="public" aria-labelledby="public-title">
<h2 id="public-title">What others can see</h2>
<div class="legal-note">
<p>Debates are public. When you debate, anyone can watch you live and in the replay afterwards: your video, your voice, your username and handle, the captions of what you say and the AI outline of your arguments. Debates can be shared by link and QR code.</p>
</div>
<table class="legal-table">
<caption>Public and private information</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Information</th>
<th scope="col">Who can see it</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Username, handle, avatar and bio</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">Everyone, including people who aren’t signed in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Debates you take part in</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">Everyone: video, voice, captions, the AI outline and your flags on it, live and in the replay.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Chat messages</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">Everyone, with your username.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Likes</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">Everyone sees the count, not who liked.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Yap Coins positions</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">Only you. Each turn’s combined results are public once revealed, without names.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Reports and appeals</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">You and our moderators.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Email, sign-in methods, notifications and analytics</th>
<td data-label="Who can see it">Only you and the staff who run YAP Arena.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Before your first debate, the Debating card in your account settings asks for your consent to recording and public replay. You can withdraw it anytime for future debates. Debates already held keep their recording, transcript and outline for the periods in <a href="#retention">How long we keep it</a>. Viewers can make their own copies of what they watch, for example by recording their screen, and we can’t control those copies.</p>
<p>A debate belongs to both debaters, so deleting your account may not delete a debate you took part in. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder decision: remove, or remove your name from, a deleted account’s debates and chat]</mark>
</p>
</section>
<section id="ai" aria-labelledby="ai-title">
<h2 id="ai-title">AI features</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Captions.</strong> A speech-to-text provider, Deepgram (or ElevenLabs, if we switch), turns the active speaker’s audio into text.</li>
<li>
<strong>AI outline.</strong> Anthropic’s Claude models summarize each turn into the arguments shown beside the debate. They receive the transcript, the debate’s question and the arguments already made, not your name, handle or email.</li>
<li>
<strong>Mistakes.</strong> Captions and outlines are automatic and can be wrong. Debaters can flag mistakes in the outline, and the flag is shown publicly.</li>
<li>
<strong>No decisions about you.</strong> The outline summarizes what was said. It doesn’t pick a winner, and it isn’t used to make decisions about you or your account.</li>
<li>
<strong>Training.</strong> We don’t use your content to train AI models. <mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm that Deepgram, ElevenLabs and Anthropic don’t train on our data under their API terms]</mark>
</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="sharing" aria-labelledby="sharing-title">
<h2 id="sharing-title">How we share it</h2>
<p>
<strong>We don’t sell or rent personal information, and we don’t share it for advertising.</strong> We share it only in these cases:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Service providers</strong> that run YAP Arena for us, listed below. Each receives only what it needs to provide its service.</li>
<li>
<strong>Everyone, for public content</strong>: the profile, debates and chat described in <a href="#public">What others can see</a>.</li>
<li>
<strong>The law and safety</strong>: when the law requires it, or to protect people’s safety, investigate abuse or defend our rights.</li>
<li>
<strong>A business change</strong>: if YAP Arena is merged, sold or reorganized, under the same protections.</li>
</ul>
<table class="legal-table">
<caption>Service providers and what each receives</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Provider</th>
<th scope="col">What it does for us</th>
<th scope="col">What it receives</th>
<th scope="col">Where</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Supabase</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Hosts our database.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Everything in our database: accounts, profiles, debates, chat, transcripts, outlines, Yap Coins records and measurement.</td>
<td data-label="Where">United States (Ohio)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Render</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Runs our website, background jobs and replay encoder.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Every request to yaparena.com, including your IP address and browser details in its request logs, and the recordings it prepares for replay.</td>
<td data-label="Where">United States (Ohio)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Auth0 (Okta)</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Signs you in with an email code or Google.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Your email address, your sign-in identifier, the IP address and browser you sign in from and, if you use Google, what Google shares with it.</td>
<td data-label="Where">United States</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Google</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">“Continue with Google”, only if you choose it.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Google confirms your email address to Auth0; YAP Arena takes only your email address from it. Google learns that you signed in to YAP Arena. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google’s privacy policy</a>.</td>
<td data-label="Where">Google’s infrastructure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Resend</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Sends the sign-in code emails for Auth0.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Your email address and the one-time code.</td>
<td data-label="Where">United States (Virginia)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">LiveKit</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Carries live video and audio, records debates and runs our transcription agent.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Debaters’ video and audio with an internal account number (not your name or email), live captions, and viewers’ connection details such as IP address.</td>
<td data-label="Where">United States</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Deepgram</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Turns debaters’ speech into text, through LiveKit.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">The audio of the speaker whose turn it is.</td>
<td data-label="Where">
<mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm region]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">ElevenLabs</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Alternative speech-to-text. Not used in production today; we may switch to it.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">The audio of the speaker whose turn it is, if we switch.</td>
<td data-label="Where">
<mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm region]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Anthropic</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Its Claude models write the AI outline of each turn.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">The transcript of each turn, the debate’s question and the arguments already made in it. Not your name, handle or email.</td>
<td data-label="Where">
<mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm region]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Cloudflare</th>
<td data-label="What it does for us">Runs our domain’s DNS, stores recordings and replays (R2) and delivers replays.</td>
<td data-label="What it receives">Recordings and replay files, and viewers’ IP address and browser details when they watch a replay.</td>
<td data-label="Where">R2 storage location <mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm location]</mark>; delivery from Cloudflare’s worldwide network</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>
<mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm each provider’s data processing terms and that none may use our data for its own purposes]</mark>
</p>
</section>
<section id="coins" aria-labelledby="coins-title">
<h2 id="coins-title">Yap Coins</h2>
<p>Yap Coins are play money with no cash value. They can’t be bought or withdrawn, so we collect no payment information. We keep a record of each account’s balance, grants and positions so the game stays fair. The combined results of each turn are public once revealed, without names.</p>
</section>
<section id="transfers" aria-labelledby="transfers-title">
<h2 id="transfers-title">Where it is stored</h2>
<p>Your information is stored and processed <strong>outside Québec and Canada</strong>. Our database and servers are in Ohio, in the United States, and our sign-in, email and video providers keep information in the United States. Our speech-to-text, AI and replay storage providers may process it in the United States or other countries <mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm Deepgram, Anthropic and Cloudflare R2 locations]</mark>. Information held abroad is subject to the laws of that country and may be accessed by its courts and authorities.</p>
<p>Before sending personal information outside Québec, we assess whether it will be adequately protected, taking into account its sensitivity, the purpose, the safeguards in place and the laws that apply, and we rely on written agreements with our providers. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder: complete the privacy impact assessment for transfers outside Québec]</mark>
</p>
</section>
<section id="retention" aria-labelledby="retention-title">
<h2 id="retention-title">How long we keep it</h2>
<table class="legal-table">
<caption>How long we keep each kind of information</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Information</th>
<th scope="col">How long</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Account, profile, follows, notifications and Debating settings</th>
<td data-label="How long">Until you delete your account.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Terms acceptance</th>
<td data-label="How long">With your account, as a record of what you accepted and when.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Sign-in session</th>
<td data-label="How long">7 days at most; signing out ends it on that device.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Sign-in steps in progress</th>
<td data-label="How long">10 minutes, used once, and deleted within the next hour.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Account activity log</th>
<td data-label="How long">90 days.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Debate recordings and replays</th>
<td data-label="How long">Kept with the debate. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder decision: retention period for recordings and replays]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Transcripts with word timings</th>
<td data-label="How long">Kept with the debate, and deleted with the speaker’s account. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder decision: retention period for transcripts]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">AI outline</th>
<td data-label="How long">Kept with the debate. A debater’s flags on it are deleted with their account.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Provider comparison transcripts</th>
<td data-label="How long">90 days. Only made while we compare two speech-to-text providers.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Transcription delivery receipts</th>
<td data-label="How long">7 days.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Chat and likes</th>
<td data-label="How long">365 days. A message that is the subject of a report is kept as long as the report.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Reports, appeals and moderation records</th>
<td data-label="How long">365 days after the decision. An open report or appeal is kept until it is decided.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Finished matchmaking queue entries</th>
<td data-label="How long">30 days.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Challenges and the debates you took part in</th>
<td data-label="How long">Kept with the debate. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder decision: retention period for challenges and debate participation]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Yap Coins records</th>
<td data-label="How long">Kept in a ledger that can’t be edited, so balances stay correct. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder decision: what happens to Yap Coins records after account deletion]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Analytics (only if you accept analytics cookies)</th>
<td data-label="How long">Events and watch sessions 90 days; an unused consent 180 days; staff reviews that classify accounts in measurement 365 days. Turning analytics off deletes that browser’s measurements at once.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Database backups</th>
<td data-label="How long">7 days, then overwritten.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Hosting and provider logs</th>
<td data-label="How long">As set by each provider. <mark class="legal-todo">[Confirm Render, LiveKit and Cloudflare log retention]</mark>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Cleanup jobs run every hour and delete information when its period ends. Deleted information can remain in database backups until they expire.</p>
</section>
<section id="cookies" aria-labelledby="cookies-title">
<h2 id="cookies-title">Cookies</h2>
<p>We use strictly necessary cookies to sign you in, keep YAP Arena secure and remember your choices. We don’t use advertising or third-party tracking cookies.</p>
<p>Our one analytics cookie recognizes your browser across visits so we can count return visits and watch time. It is off by default: it is set only if you choose <strong>Accept all</strong> or turn on Analytics, and we treat your browser’s Global Privacy Control signal as a refusal.</p>
<p>The <a href="/cookies">Cookie Policy</a> lists every cookie and how long it lasts. Change your choice anytime with <strong>Cookie settings</strong> at the bottom of any page.</p>
</section>
<section id="rights" aria-labelledby="rights-title">
<h2 id="rights-title">Your rights and choices</h2>
<p>You can ask to access the personal information we hold about you, to correct it, to receive it in a structured, commonly used format, to stop its use or have it deleted, and to have links to it de-indexed where the law allows. You can also withdraw a consent you gave.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Download your data</strong> from your account’s Privacy settings: a file with your profile, sign-in methods, follows, notifications, terms acceptance and account activity. Chat, reports, debate participation and Yap Coins records aren’t in the file yet; ask us for them.</li>
<li>
<strong>Correct your profile</strong> in your account settings. Your handle is permanent; ask us if it must change.</li>
<li>
<strong>Turn analytics cookies off</strong> with Cookie settings; it deletes that browser’s measurements.</li>
<li>
<strong>Withdraw debating consent</strong> in the Debating card in your account settings.</li>
<li>
<strong>Delete your account</strong> by emailing <mark class="legal-todo">[Privacy email]</mark> from the address you sign in with. Deletion is done by hand for now: we confirm the request, end your sessions, delete your profile and sign-in details, and delete or de-identify the other information tied to your account, except what the periods above say we keep. <mark class="legal-todo">[Founder decision: account deletion rules (prompt 12c)]</mark>
</li>
</ul>
<p>We answer requests within 30 days. We may ask you to confirm your identity first.</p>
</section>
<section id="security" aria-labelledby="security-title">
<h2 id="security-title">How we protect it</h2>
<p>Connections use HTTPS. We don’t store passwords; sign-in uses one-time email codes or Google through Auth0. Sessions are random values that we can end at any time, and staff access is limited by role. Recordings are stored privately and served only through short-lived signed links.</p>
<p>No system is perfectly secure. If a confidentiality incident creates a risk of serious injury, we will notify the Commission d’accès à l’information and the people affected, and we keep a register of incidents.</p>
</section>
<section id="age" aria-labelledby="age-title">
<h2 id="age-title">Age</h2>
<p>YAP Arena is for people 18 and older. We don’t knowingly collect information from anyone younger. If we learn that an account belongs to someone under 18, we close it and delete its information. Contact us at <mark class="legal-todo">[Privacy email]</mark> if you believe this has happened.</p>
</section>
<section id="governance" aria-labelledby="governance-title">
<h2 id="governance-title">How we manage personal information</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Who is responsible.</strong> The person in charge of the protection of personal information (see <a href="#who">Who we are</a>) approves how we collect, use, keep and destroy personal information, and handles requests and complaints.</li>
<li>
<strong>Access.</strong> Staff and contractors see personal information only when their role needs it. Moderation and operator tools need a role we grant on purpose.</li>
<li>
<strong>Keeping and destroying.</strong> We keep information only for the periods in <a href="#retention">How long we keep it</a>, then delete it or remove what identifies you.</li>
<li>
<strong>New projects.</strong> We assess privacy risks before a new project involving personal information, and before sending it outside Québec.</li>
<li>
<strong>Complaints.</strong> Write to <mark class="legal-todo">[Privacy email]</mark>. We acknowledge your complaint, look into it and answer in writing within 30 days.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="changes" aria-labelledby="changes-title">
<h2 id="changes-title">Changes to this policy</h2>
<p>When we change this policy, we post the new version here with its date. If a change affects how we use information you have already given us, we tell you in YAP Arena before it applies and ask for your consent again where the law requires it.</p>
</section>
<section id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
<h2 id="contact-title">Contact and complaints</h2>
<p>Questions, requests and complaints about privacy go to <mark class="legal-todo">[Name and title of the person in charge]</mark> at <mark class="legal-todo">[Privacy email]</mark>, or by mail to <mark class="legal-todo">[Legal name of the business]</mark>, <mark class="legal-todo">[Business address in Québec]</mark>.</p>
<p>If you aren’t satisfied with our answer, you can complain to the <a href="https://www.cai.gouv.qc.ca">Commission d’accès à l’information du Québec</a> or to the <a href="https://www.priv.gc.ca">Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada</a>.</p>
</section>
<a class="legal-top" href="#main">Back to top <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span>
</a>
</article>
</div>
</div>
</main>
